def build(course, m):
    return course(92, 'Fisheries Livelihoods, Labour and Social Protection',
        'Fisheries Livelihoods,\nLabour and\nSocial Protection',
        'Assess work and protection gaps across a fictional small-scale fishery and design a traceable service referral.', [
        m('Recognise visible and hidden fish work',
            'Map fisheries work, earnings and gaps in worker representation.',
            """
Map who prepares equipment and supplies, catches, transports, processes and sells fish. Work may be occasional, informal, seasonal or unpaid. An owner register may be useful for licensing questions, while a labour enquiry needs information about tasks, hours and control of income. Include arrangements in which someone receives a catch share or receives fish for household use. [S81]

Describe access to inputs and the ability to refuse unfair terms. A processor may have a regular buyer yet lack cold storage and have little time to negotiate after landing. A boat crew member may face income gaps when weather prevents trips. Ask who sets the terms, whether workers understand them and which authorised route can address a concern. [S81]

The global fisheries study estimates catch, nutrition, landed value, employment and livelihoods from different data sources and methods. The researchers examine roles across value-chain stages, which helps make work visible beyond harvesting. Use that insight to design a local set of questions about tasks and pay. Record the source and period for any measure used in a fictional profile. [R91]

When an association represents owners, add routes for crew, shore workers and self-employed traders to raise their priorities. Pay attention to meeting times, language and accessibility. Ask which decision participants could influence, and document disputes or missing voices. Imagine two people sharing the same stall but paying different fees under different business arrangements. A single average may hide an important difference. [R92] Consider changes between a profitable landing period and a lean season. The same person may shift roles or combine work with unpaid care.
            """,
            'The only livelihood register lists boat owners. A proposed stall charge affects informal traders and processors who did not receive meeting notices.',
            'Create a worker map and propose a way to capture terms, work contributions and concerns across the chain.',
            'Record actual tasks and payment arrangements. Consult shore workers through an accessible route and identify the decision owner.',
            """
Describe tasks across the chain.|Who is directly missed by a register restricted to owners?|A shore processor without a boat.|Every registered owner.|A licensor using the owner list.|A registered owner who attends meetings.|Ownership data can omit work in other roles.
Record forms of payment.|How should regular payment in fish enter an earnings inquiry?|Record the arrangement and its role in income or consumption.|Count it automatically as a boat asset.|Discard it from any livelihood inquiry.|Record it as an unpaid tax.|Payment in kind can matter to household livelihoods.
Distinguish source measures.|Which source measure is closest to first-sale value?|Catch multiplied by first-sale prices using stated scope.|Number of people using an appeals desk.|Number of unpaid duties.|Meals consumed by each household.|Landed value describes transactions at a defined stage.
Identify bargaining constraints.|Which situation can weaken a trader's bargaining position?|Spoilage risk limits the time available to compare buyers.|Two equally accessible buyers offer stable terms.|The trader can safely store every lot.|Terms are written and reviewable.|Time pressure and limited options affect negotiation.
Represent excluded workers.|Which step helps include people affected by the stall charge?|Consult informal users before the decision and explain how comments will be considered.|Invite only registered owners after fees are final.|Ask the project manager to speak for all traders.|Count every social media view as consultation.|Representation requires a practical opportunity to influence decisions.
            """),
        m('Connect decent work to practical support',
            'Identify a worker concern and the service or competent role that can address it.',
            """
Decent work concerns the circumstances under which people earn a livelihood. In a fishery, distinct concerns can include uncertain pay, long hours, occupational exposures, limited representation or inadequate access to protection. Begin by separating what the worker reports from the issue a responsible service can assess. For example, a report of repeated payment delays calls for fact collection and an authorised advice route. [S81]

A worker using a communal processing area may have concerns about air quality, burns or the burden of handling materials. The social work role includes understanding the effect on daily life, connecting people to appropriate support and raising shared concerns with authorised teams. Use published agency channels for real emergencies and qualified safety and health assessment for technical conclusions. [S81]

Analyse effects on workers with different power and opportunities. Missing pay records can affect a claim. A neutral conversation may still be hard if the employer is present or housing depends on maintaining the job. Give workers a private, accessible means to ask for support. Identify a trusted authorised referral role, practical language needs and what can be shared with consent and appropriate authority. [S81]

Make a classroom referral note with the concern, source, immediate need, just enough supporting information and the date follow-up is due. Name the origin and destination of the referral. An agreed next contact can be more valuable than a long list of agencies. Keep unresolved issues open until someone confirms receipt and an accessible response. Where protection barriers affect multiple workers, record that pattern. [S81] Check whether the receiving service has a safe appointment route before telling the worker when to expect a response.
            """,
            'A processor reports two missed payments and asks for help privately. The group manager proposes discussing the case in front of every buyer.',
            'Identify a confidential first response and a referral that can check payment records and applicable rights.',
            'Record the concern and safe contact option. Refer through an authorised advice or labour route and track a response.',
            """
Classify the work concern.|Which evidence helps assess the reported wage delay?|The applicable terms and records showing due dates and payments.|Only regional catch projections.|The latest auction price alone.|The typeface in the worker register.|Verification needs the relevant terms and events.
Route technical assessment.|What should a practitioner do about a reported processing exposure that affects work?|Connect the worker to appropriate health or safety assessment and record the concern.|Determine the exposure level from a distance.|Certify the facility as safe.|Prescribe a treatment based on the report.|Technical health and safety judgements need qualified assessment.
Protect the discussion.|Which meeting arrangement best supports this worker's stated preference?|A private accessible conversation with a safe follow-up option.|A public discussion with the buyers.|A social media post naming the concern.|An anonymous poll that publishes every comment.|Privacy makes it easier to seek support safely.
Assign responsibility.|What helps an authorised labour referral move forward?|A responsible receiving role with limited relevant information.|Publishing the whole account in an open group.|A general instruction to ask elsewhere.|Treating the note itself as payment.|The receiving role needs a clear purpose and relevant facts.
Check progression.|What should the practitioner record at review?|Receipt, response and any outstanding service barrier.|Only the count of outgoing messages.|The worker's assumed satisfaction.|A definitive ruling without evidence.|Follow-up tests whether the person reached appropriate assistance.
            """),
        m('Track access to social protection',
            'Compare effective protection before and after a fictional fishing interruption.',
            """
Social protection can help people manage risks that interrupt livelihood, such as injury or loss of earnings. Examine the protections applicable to the case and the actions people must take to reach them. The FAO guidance asks states to consider the needs of small-scale fisheries workers across the chain. A real eligibility decision depends on programme rules and the competent service. [S81]

Use an access pathway with stages for awareness, application, decision, timely receipt and a correction or review route. People can be excluded at each stage. A document requirement can be particularly difficult for workers whose earnings are irregular or informal. Track who could understand the notice, submit acceptable evidence and receive help within a useful period. Record where data are missing. [S81]

Consider fictional counts of thirty eligible workers and eighteen recorded applications. If twelve people receive timely support, that is twelve of thirty among eligible workers and twelve of eighteen among applicants. Both measures are useful when labelled. The latter figure omits gaps before application. Do not assume that those who did not apply had adequate income or chose to stay outside. [S81]

Cooperatives can offer information or mutual support, and membership terms can exclude workers. The Nigerian study of cooperative membership and adaptation uses survey observations and models that depend on assumptions. Compare access among members and nonmembers in the exercise, and ask whether prior influence or resources help explain any difference. Propose one practical improvement with an owner, timing and testable measure. [R93] If someone receives assistance late, record their experience separately. A delayed payment can still matter without meeting a timeliness measure.
            """,
            'After a fictional interruption, 30 workers meet supplied eligibility rules, 18 applied and 12 received timely support. The agency reports success using only applicants.',
            'Compute both rates and identify a possible barrier at application. Propose a support step and an accountable follow-up measure.',
            'Timely receipt is 40 percent among the eligible group and about 67 percent among applicants. Find out why eligible workers did not apply.',
            """
Separate entitlement from receipt.|Which record shows effective timely access in the classroom case?|Support actually reached a worker in the useful period.|An eligibility rule was announced.|A worker opened the application form.|A committee planned to publish a notice.|Receipt requires actual delivery within the relevant time.
Keep eligible people in view.|What proportion of all eligible workers received timely support?|40 percent.|60 percent.|About 67 percent.|12 percent.|Twelve divided by thirty gives 0.4.
Use the right denominator.|What does 12 out of 18 describe?|The timely receipt rate among applicants.|The timely receipt rate among all eligible workers.|The share of all fisheries households in the district.|The application rate among eligible workers.|The denominator is the application stage.
Examine cooperative membership.|Which concern belongs in a cooperative-based offer?|Whether nonmembers have a practical service route.|Whether a cooperative label establishes universal access.|Whether the meeting can replace all formal checks.|Whether each reported effect applies identically across states.|Membership rules affect who can benefit.
Give a measurable correction.|Which improvement supports a fair trial?|Offer staffed documentation help and check timely receipt by route.|Switch to online-only entry and report page views.|Count new flyers as payments.|Use a meeting photo as full coverage evidence.|An improvement should address a barrier and track the resulting service outcome.
            """),
    ])
